template: revtex-pra
metadata:
  title: Complete Classification of Full State Revivals on Separable Quantum Walks on Tori
  authors:
  - name: Ian Craig
    email: ian.craig@atu.ie
    affiliation: Atlantic Technological University, Sligo, Ireland
  - name: Michael McGetterick
    affiliation: University of Galway, Galway, Ireland
  abstract: 'We present a complete classification of full-state revivals for
    separable discrete-time quantum walks on $k \times m$ toroidal
    lattices. To achieve this, we prove (up to a global phase) that a
    2D torus exhibits full-state revivals whenever both spatial
    dimensions satisfy $k, m \in \{2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10\}$ and their
    respective revival period sets share a common element,
    $\mathcal{N}_k \cap \mathcal{N}_m \neq \emptyset$; our exhaustive
    computational search over $k, m \le 16$ finds no revival outside
    this class. Our methodology builds on Dukes'' results
    \cite{Dukes2014} on 1D cycles for a general coin with reflection
    parameter $\rho$ and phase parameter $\delta$. We extend this
    framework to a four-parameter general coin on the $k \times m$
    torus. We report a computational search that enumerates all
    full-state revivals for $k \times m$ toroidal lattices with
    dimensions in the above revival set. We identify three phenomena
    in the 2D case: (i) multi-period revivals on the same torus size,
    where full-state revivals occur across multiple distinct step
    counts depending on the coin parameters (e.g. the $3 \times 3$
    torus); (ii) mixed-$\rho$ parameters, where revivals occur under
    unequal reflection parameters along the two axes; (iii)
    mixed-$\delta$ asymmetry, where revivals occur under coin phase
    parameters along the two axes ($\delta_x \neq \delta_y$).
    Notably, the longest revival period, $N = 60$, occurs on the
    $5 \times 5$ torus. This paper provides a full classification of
    full-state revivals given the four parameters of the coin and may
    find applications in quantum memory and in structures such as
    frequency dividers and counters.'
  date: today
  bibliographystyle: apsrev4-2
  bibliography: docs/sep-coin-tori/references.bib
